{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-142938",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3},\\,{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(-2,1),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-1,4),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(5,2),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =-2+(-1)+5 \\\\  & =2, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =1+4+2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} & ∼N(2,7).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
